1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,000
Spiraling deep into the earth.

2
00:00:04,001 --> 00:00:08,000
Searching for water, oil, and knowledge.

3
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:16,000
Grinding, crushing, and scraping the way through earth, steel, ice, or stone.

4
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:20,000
Now, drilling on Modern Marvels.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,000
The New Mexico desert.

6
00:00:37,001 --> 00:00:43,000
This may seem an unlikely place to drill for water,

7
00:00:43,001 --> 00:00:49,000
but that's precisely what the Eads drilling team is here to do.

8
00:00:49,001 --> 00:00:52,000
Level the rig up good and straight.

9
00:00:52,001 --> 00:00:56,000
That way, when we're drilling, we maintain a level hole.

10
00:00:56,001 --> 00:00:59,600
we're not getting crooked and causing a problem.

11
00:01:03,600 --> 00:01:06,840
With his rig set up, Alan Eads lowers the first

12
00:01:06,940 --> 00:01:09,940
of the many drill bits into place and starts breaking ground.

13
00:01:19,460 --> 00:01:23,760
Water well drilling is a $3 billion a year industry.

14
00:01:23,761 --> 00:01:28,060
In the United States alone, water drillers create 800,000

15
00:01:28,160 --> 00:01:30,240
new wells annually.

16
00:01:30,340 --> 00:01:33,040
When they drill, they usually find water.

17
00:01:35,140 --> 00:01:37,600
You can't tell by looking at the surface,

18
00:01:37,700 --> 00:01:40,580
but this semi-arid region sits atop one of the largest

19
00:01:40,680 --> 00:01:45,580
aquifers, or underground water reserves, in the United States,

20
00:01:45,680 --> 00:01:53,760
the 174,000-square-mile Ogallala Aquifer.

21
00:01:53,761 --> 00:01:59,260
This puts the odds of striking water in the EADS team's favor.

22
00:01:59,360 --> 00:02:03,000
They'll have to clear approximately 6 feet of topsoil,

23
00:02:03,100 --> 00:02:07,080
100 feet of various limestone and sandstone rock,

24
00:02:07,180 --> 00:02:10,940
and 10 or more feet of sand before they can expect to reach

25
00:02:11,040 --> 00:02:12,080
the water table.

26
00:02:16,480 --> 00:02:19,560
Within 20 minutes of drilling, the team hits rock,

27
00:02:19,660 --> 00:02:23,760
and it's time to change out the drill bit.

28
00:02:23,761 --> 00:02:25,260
This is our down hole hammer.

29
00:02:25,360 --> 00:02:28,560
It's run off the air compressor of the drilling rig.

30
00:02:28,660 --> 00:02:30,760
It's an 8-inch bit.

31
00:02:30,860 --> 00:02:35,700
It'll cut rock a lot faster than any other method.

32
00:02:35,800 --> 00:02:39,200
It has sped the industry up tremendously

33
00:02:39,300 --> 00:02:42,220
in hard rock drilling.

34
00:02:42,320 --> 00:02:45,020
This down hole hammer works by thrusting into the rock

35
00:02:45,120 --> 00:02:49,020
up to 1,500 times per minute as the drill string, or drill

36
00:02:49,120 --> 00:02:51,020
pipe, is rotating.

37
00:02:51,120 --> 00:02:53,060
Pretty much, it's a one-man operation

38
00:02:53,061 --> 00:02:56,400
during the drilling process once you get set up.

39
00:03:00,160 --> 00:03:02,740
The EADS team's drilling rig has come a long way

40
00:03:02,840 --> 00:03:05,840
from the earliest well drilling, done in ancient China

41
00:03:05,940 --> 00:03:09,740
around 600 BC.

42
00:03:09,840 --> 00:03:12,980
They use a method of percussion drilling

43
00:03:13,080 --> 00:03:15,880
that they call the spring pole.

44
00:03:15,980 --> 00:03:19,960
The spring pole is just simply a branch or a tree itself

45
00:03:20,060 --> 00:03:22,060
with a fulcrum, like a teeter-totter.

46
00:03:22,061 --> 00:03:25,000
but they put the fulcrum on closer to one end

47
00:03:25,060 --> 00:03:28,300
so that the other end stuck up high in the air.

48
00:03:28,360 --> 00:03:31,640
And then they'd produce a reciprocating motion

49
00:03:31,700 --> 00:03:34,140
that raised and lowered the bit.

50
00:03:34,200 --> 00:03:37,880
And that pulverized the formation.

51
00:03:37,940 --> 00:03:40,380
Lagging behind, Europeans were still

52
00:03:40,440 --> 00:03:44,480
digging their wells by hand until 1100 AD.

53
00:03:44,540 --> 00:03:47,260
There were a number of wells drilled in Western Europe

54
00:03:47,320 --> 00:03:49,580
after the first millennium.

55
00:03:49,660 --> 00:03:51,180
And some of them were very deep.

56
00:03:51,181 --> 00:03:53,820
They used primitive percussion methods.

57
00:03:53,920 --> 00:03:56,760
A famous one at Artois in France

58
00:03:56,860 --> 00:03:59,160
was drilled in the 11th century.

59
00:03:59,260 --> 00:04:04,400
It went over 1,000 feet deep.

60
00:04:04,500 --> 00:04:08,000
Famed 15th century artist and inventor Leonardo da Vinci

61
00:04:08,100 --> 00:04:10,560
sketched a design for a radically different well-drilling

62
00:04:10,660 --> 00:04:13,500
apparatus.

63
00:04:13,600 --> 00:04:15,240
The principle was simple.

64
00:04:15,340 --> 00:04:19,480
It used a sharp rotating bit to delve into the earth.

65
00:04:19,481 --> 00:04:21,520
But it wasn't until the mid-19th century

66
00:04:21,620 --> 00:04:23,680
that rotary drilling became a viable option

67
00:04:23,780 --> 00:04:26,220
for drilling deep wells.

68
00:04:26,320 --> 00:04:28,720
In the 1840s in England, they had the idea

69
00:04:28,820 --> 00:04:30,220
they would circulate fluid down.

70
00:04:30,320 --> 00:04:31,620
And as they circulated the fluid,

71
00:04:31,720 --> 00:04:35,060
they would bring the rocks back out, the advantage of being

72
00:04:35,160 --> 00:04:37,640
continuous as opposed to a batch process.

73
00:04:37,740 --> 00:04:41,040
And so your efficiencies went way up.

74
00:04:41,140 --> 00:04:42,640
Rotary drilling is the method used

75
00:04:42,740 --> 00:04:46,780
to drill 85% of all deep wells in the United States,

76
00:04:46,880 --> 00:04:49,240
including this one, being drilled by the East

77
00:04:49,241 --> 00:04:53,240
team. They sometimes employ a percussive element, like the

78
00:04:53,340 --> 00:04:55,240
downhole hammer.

79
00:04:59,080 --> 00:05:01,980
One hour into the drilling, they've passed the rock layer

80
00:05:02,080 --> 00:05:04,920
and changed the drill bit once again.

81
00:05:05,020 --> 00:05:07,720
This is our drill collar.

82
00:05:07,820 --> 00:05:11,560
We run in on our final trip to go to bottom.

83
00:05:11,660 --> 00:05:13,600
The drill collar consists of a drill bit

84
00:05:13,700 --> 00:05:15,660
attached to a weighted pipe.

85
00:05:15,661 --> 00:05:20,540
This drill collar weighs approximately 1,800 pounds.

86
00:05:21,540 --> 00:05:27,840
It just more weight on the bottom of the string, drill string, keeps the hole straight.

87
00:05:28,200 --> 00:05:28,900
Ready?

88
00:05:29,020 --> 00:05:31,440
We've got to add more pipe to get deeper.

89
00:05:31,580 --> 00:05:40,400
Our carousel, with our pipe rack over on the side, we can go up to 300 feet with what pipe we carry on the rig.

90
00:05:40,401 --> 00:05:47,400
Our deepest wells here are around 250 to 260 feet.

91
00:05:47,401 --> 00:05:49,400
Don't want that.

92
00:05:49,401 --> 00:05:50,400
That's two?

93
00:05:50,401 --> 00:05:51,400
Yeah.

94
00:05:51,401 --> 00:05:53,400
Two to 696.

95
00:06:03,400 --> 00:06:06,400
Now that they're drilling into sand,

96
00:06:06,401 --> 00:06:08,940
They introduce a special water polymer mixture.

97
00:06:14,040 --> 00:06:18,840
The mixture is pumped through the drill bit and into the hole.

98
00:06:18,940 --> 00:06:21,980
The mud created forces the cuttings up to the surface

99
00:06:22,080 --> 00:06:23,680
and also stabilizes the well.

100
00:06:28,080 --> 00:06:31,200
That's the water sand we just hit.

101
00:06:31,300 --> 00:06:34,000
Started the rattling and getting into gravel.

102
00:06:34,001 --> 00:06:36,660
It's just a lot coarser sand.

103
00:06:36,760 --> 00:06:40,140
We hit it probably at about 110 feet.

104
00:06:40,240 --> 00:06:42,500
But I think this is going to be a good well hitting it that

105
00:06:42,600 --> 00:06:45,140
shallow.

106
00:06:45,240 --> 00:06:47,340
Not even an hour and a half into the drilling,

107
00:06:47,440 --> 00:06:49,740
and they hit the water table.

108
00:06:49,840 --> 00:06:51,280
But Allen still needs to continue

109
00:06:51,380 --> 00:06:53,660
to drill for another 100 feet or so

110
00:06:53,760 --> 00:06:57,360
until he hits the bottom of the aquifer.

111
00:06:57,460 --> 00:07:01,420
The last 10 years, probably 80% of our work

112
00:07:01,421 --> 00:07:04,020
has been replacing wells that weren't drilled all the way

113
00:07:04,120 --> 00:07:05,820
to the bottom of the aquifer.

114
00:07:05,920 --> 00:07:09,820
The aquifer's just slowly dropped in level,

115
00:07:09,920 --> 00:07:12,360
and those wells have started going dry.

116
00:07:12,460 --> 00:07:15,660
So now, nearly all the new wells we do,

117
00:07:15,760 --> 00:07:20,280
we go to the bottom of the aquifer.

118
00:07:20,380 --> 00:07:22,680
Three and a half hours into the operation

119
00:07:22,780 --> 00:07:25,880
and approaching 225 feet, the drillers

120
00:07:25,980 --> 00:07:28,920
anticipate hitting the bottom at any moment.

121
00:07:28,921 --> 00:07:32,260
That red clay is the bottom of the aquifer now.

122
00:07:32,261 --> 00:07:34,060
We'll start flushing the hole out,

123
00:07:34,061 --> 00:07:35,560
see if we get some clear water.

124
00:07:38,960 --> 00:07:39,760
There we go.

125
00:07:43,160 --> 00:07:45,560
We have water.

126
00:07:45,561 --> 00:07:46,580
Nice and clean.

127
00:07:50,180 --> 00:07:54,920
The town we live in is approximately 30,000 people.

128
00:07:54,921 --> 00:07:58,220
Everywhere I go, people know who I am

129
00:07:58,221 --> 00:08:01,860
Because I'm the water well guy.

130
00:08:01,960 --> 00:08:04,020
Today, the profession of water well drilling

131
00:08:04,120 --> 00:08:06,660
is usually overshadowed by another, more lucrative

132
00:08:06,760 --> 00:08:12,840
business that it helped to spawn, the oil industry.

133
00:08:12,940 --> 00:08:15,200
Water well drilling, brine drilling, and so forth,

134
00:08:15,300 --> 00:08:17,500
is obviously very critical to humanity.

135
00:08:17,600 --> 00:08:19,040
We need water.

136
00:08:19,140 --> 00:08:22,140
But oil is one of those commodities

137
00:08:22,240 --> 00:08:25,040
that people are willing to pay a lot for, obviously.

138
00:08:25,041 --> 00:08:29,040
And so, if you will, the money follows where that is.

139
00:08:29,140 --> 00:08:30,780
And so, if you will, the technology

140
00:08:30,880 --> 00:08:33,280
is in the oil and gas industry, because that's where you can

141
00:08:33,380 --> 00:08:36,560
afford to do the research and the development of the various

142
00:08:36,660 --> 00:08:41,060
tools that we use to develop and drill wells.

143
00:08:41,160 --> 00:08:44,560
On August 27, 1859, Edwin Drake

144
00:08:44,660 --> 00:08:47,100
drilled the first oil well in the United States,

145
00:08:47,200 --> 00:08:50,140
in Titusville, Pennsylvania.

146
00:08:50,240 --> 00:08:53,200
Local water well drillers had opened the way for him.

147
00:08:53,201 --> 00:08:56,900
While looking for water, they'd struck the viscous black liquid,

148
00:08:57,000 --> 00:09:00,800
so Drake knew exactly where to drill.

149
00:09:00,900 --> 00:09:04,540
Their spoils became Drake's treasure.

150
00:09:04,640 --> 00:09:08,040
And from there, leaving water well drillers in their dust,

151
00:09:08,140 --> 00:09:09,960
the oil drilling revolution began.

152
00:09:14,060 --> 00:09:17,260
Today's oil well drilling is a long way from the early days,

153
00:09:17,360 --> 00:09:19,260
when secondhand water well technology

154
00:09:19,360 --> 00:09:23,200
was used to drill for crude.

155
00:09:23,201 --> 00:09:27,240
In the Gulf of Mexico resides one of the world's largest and

156
00:09:27,340 --> 00:09:29,840
most sophisticated drill rigs.

157
00:09:29,940 --> 00:09:31,100
Its mission?

158
00:09:31,200 --> 00:09:35,140
To tap into oil deposits created by decaying life forms that

159
00:09:35,240 --> 00:09:38,220
populated the region 200 million years ago.

160
00:09:38,320 --> 00:09:41,260
Deposits potentially buried hundreds of feet beneath the

161
00:09:41,360 --> 00:09:43,280
sea floor.

162
00:09:43,380 --> 00:09:47,520
The discoverer Deep Seas is a fifth generation floating

163
00:09:47,620 --> 00:09:51,120
drilling rig, or actually a drill ship.

164
00:09:51,121 --> 00:09:57,120
It's 835 feet long and 125 feet wide and boasts not one,

165
00:09:57,220 --> 00:10:00,120
but two drill systems in a single derrick.

166
00:10:00,220 --> 00:10:01,960
As this rig floats on the sea, we

167
00:10:02,060 --> 00:10:04,760
can drill in 10,000-foot water depth.

168
00:10:04,860 --> 00:10:08,400
So that means basically we've got 10,000 feet of water

169
00:10:08,500 --> 00:10:11,800
before we ever get to ground and ever actually

170
00:10:11,900 --> 00:10:15,240
get to break ground with a drill bit.

171
00:10:15,340 --> 00:10:18,140
The deep seas utilizes the most up-to-date rotary

172
00:10:18,141 --> 00:10:21,680
rotary drilling technology in the world.

173
00:10:21,681 --> 00:10:25,280
Right now, we're drilling an 18 and 1 1 1⁄8 inch hole.

174
00:10:25,281 --> 00:10:28,880
As you can see, the drill string right here is rotating.

175
00:10:28,881 --> 00:10:32,540
As the drill string rotates, it rotates the bit down the hole,

176
00:10:32,541 --> 00:10:37,040
anywhere between 150 and 200 rotations per minute.

177
00:10:37,041 --> 00:10:38,940
Right here, we have what we call a PRS.

178
00:10:38,941 --> 00:10:40,840
It's a pipe racking system.

179
00:10:40,841 --> 00:10:42,980
And this is the mechanization that you

180
00:10:42,981 --> 00:10:44,680
see on board an offshore rig that you

181
00:10:44,681 --> 00:10:47,200
don't see on a typical land operation.

182
00:10:47,201 --> 00:10:50,240
the PRS will come in and pick up a piece of drill pipe.

183
00:10:50,241 --> 00:10:52,740
It'll move it over and stab it in,

184
00:10:52,741 --> 00:10:54,740
though people actually need to touch this equipment.

185
00:10:58,180 --> 00:11:01,440
From the 135-foot-long drill string assemblies

186
00:11:01,441 --> 00:11:05,860
to the 226-foot-tall derrick, everything on this drill rig

187
00:11:05,861 --> 00:11:10,120
is bigger and better, including the drill bits themselves.

188
00:11:10,121 --> 00:11:12,500
We've got two different types of bits here.

189
00:11:12,501 --> 00:11:15,720
This is a tricone bit, sometimes called a roller cone bit.

190
00:11:15,721 --> 00:11:18,380
This bit, as you can see, each of these cones

191
00:11:18,381 --> 00:11:19,360
individually moves.

192
00:11:19,800 --> 00:11:22,920
The way this bit will drill is you'll run it on the bottom

193
00:11:22,921 --> 00:11:23,740
of the drill string.

194
00:11:24,220 --> 00:11:27,640
As it sits down on the formation and you start rotating

195
00:11:27,641 --> 00:11:31,160
the drill pipe, these tend to move around as it rolls around.

196
00:11:31,400 --> 00:11:34,280
And it rolls around and actually breaks up the formation.

197
00:11:36,020 --> 00:11:39,620
The father of an American icon developed the roller cone bit

198
00:11:39,621 --> 00:11:41,020
early in the 20th century.

199
00:11:41,480 --> 00:11:43,300
A guy named by the name of Howard Hughes, Sr.

200
00:11:43,301 --> 00:11:44,680
came up with that in 1908.

201
00:11:44,681 --> 00:11:47,780
And what he did is he actually only had two.

202
00:11:47,781 --> 00:11:51,220
The tricone actually didn't develop until about early 1930s.

203
00:11:51,221 --> 00:11:54,920
The newest drilling bits are called

204
00:11:54,921 --> 00:11:58,360
polycrystalline diamond cutter bits, or PDCs.

205
00:11:58,361 --> 00:12:00,400
Out here in deep water, we typically

206
00:12:00,401 --> 00:12:02,740
use the more expensive PDC bits.

207
00:12:02,741 --> 00:12:06,800
The PDC bit, the way it cuts is through dragging or a scraping

208
00:12:06,801 --> 00:12:08,400
or a scouring action.

209
00:12:08,401 --> 00:12:11,140
And you can actually see the type of cuttings

210
00:12:11,141 --> 00:12:12,400
that it will bring up.

211
00:12:12,401 --> 00:12:17,560
bring up, as you can see, as it scrapes around the bottom of the hole.

212
00:12:17,561 --> 00:12:19,400
Here you can see where the nozzles are.

213
00:12:19,401 --> 00:12:22,720
The nozzles are what actually transports the drilling fluid out

214
00:12:22,721 --> 00:12:24,220
from the drill string.

215
00:12:24,221 --> 00:12:27,080
It'll come around and come down through these channels.

216
00:12:27,081 --> 00:12:32,820
And these nozzles are actually sized depending on the application.

217
00:12:32,821 --> 00:12:34,640
Today, drillers are no longer committed

218
00:12:34,641 --> 00:12:36,480
to drilling a vertical hole.

219
00:12:36,481 --> 00:12:38,980
Chevron right now is paying about half a million dollars

220
00:12:38,981 --> 00:12:40,900
a day to be out here drilling.

221
00:12:40,901 --> 00:12:43,840
In order to maximize that and get to our target

222
00:12:43,841 --> 00:12:46,880
quickly and efficiently, we use high-tech tools.

223
00:12:46,881 --> 00:12:50,420
One of the tools we use is called a rotary steerable system.

224
00:12:50,421 --> 00:12:53,720
For a rotary steerable system, what it allows us to do

225
00:12:53,721 --> 00:12:55,940
is to be able to rotate the drill string

226
00:12:55,941 --> 00:12:58,600
and change direction in the hole at the same time.

227
00:12:58,601 --> 00:13:01,480
And as this thing is rotating, those pads

228
00:13:01,481 --> 00:13:04,140
will pop out as the drill string moves.

229
00:13:04,141 --> 00:13:06,440
And as you want to try to nudge the bit to the left

230
00:13:06,441 --> 00:13:09,560
or to the right to be able to build angle

231
00:13:09,561 --> 00:13:12,120
to get to our target.

232
00:13:12,121 --> 00:13:15,140
To determine where to drill, the Chevron team members

233
00:13:15,141 --> 00:13:17,500
are armed with the latest in seismic 3D technology.

234
00:13:20,880 --> 00:13:23,080
Hundreds of miles away in Houston,

235
00:13:23,081 --> 00:13:25,840
they have an extended network of geologists and engineers

236
00:13:25,841 --> 00:13:28,880
at their beck and call.

237
00:13:28,881 --> 00:13:33,420
And 10,000 feet underwater, they have remote operated vehicles,

238
00:13:33,421 --> 00:13:35,720
giving them a glimpse of what's happening on the seafloor.

239
00:13:35,721 --> 00:13:39,720
But even with all of these high-tech tools,

240
00:13:39,820 --> 00:13:42,520
striking oil remains hit or miss.

241
00:13:42,620 --> 00:13:46,360
Still, with all the technology found aboard the deep seas,

242
00:13:46,460 --> 00:13:48,760
the cards are definitely stacked in their favor.

243
00:13:52,100 --> 00:13:55,480
Others are winning as well, as oil drilling innovations

244
00:13:55,580 --> 00:13:59,380
find groundbreaking uses in the arena of scientific drilling.

245
00:14:05,720 --> 00:14:12,320
At the Japanese port of Nagasaki,

246
00:14:12,960 --> 00:14:14,660
the largest and most sophisticated

247
00:14:14,661 --> 00:14:16,360
of all scientific drill ships

248
00:14:16,361 --> 00:14:17,760
has recently been unveiled.

249
00:14:20,360 --> 00:14:22,400
It's called the Chikyu.

250
00:14:24,140 --> 00:14:26,580
It's 660 feet long,

251
00:14:26,660 --> 00:14:28,320
more than two football fields in length.

252
00:14:28,520 --> 00:14:30,100
It's got a drilling derrick on board

253
00:14:30,101 --> 00:14:31,780
that's the tallest derrick in the world.

254
00:14:31,781 --> 00:14:35,420
It's something like 370 feet high

255
00:14:35,421 --> 00:14:36,460
above the water line.

256
00:14:36,620 --> 00:14:38,400
That's taller than the Statue of Liberty.

257
00:14:39,440 --> 00:14:43,360
The mammoth 57,500-ton vessel dwarfs

258
00:14:43,361 --> 00:14:45,760
all other rigs used in scientific exploration.

259
00:14:48,260 --> 00:14:50,820
When fully assembled, the drill pipe

260
00:14:50,821 --> 00:14:52,380
is nearly six miles long,

261
00:14:53,320 --> 00:14:56,380
about 22 times the height of the Empire State Building.

262
00:14:57,100 --> 00:14:58,780
The Chikyu is the latest incarnation

263
00:14:58,781 --> 00:15:00,500
of scientific ocean drilling vessel,

264
00:15:00,700 --> 00:15:02,460
and it's designed to be able to drill

265
00:15:02,461 --> 00:15:05,780
as much as four or five miles beneath the seafloor

266
00:15:05,781 --> 00:15:07,860
in order to get at parts of the Earth's crust,

267
00:15:07,960 --> 00:15:10,800
the plate tectonics and the upper part of the Earth's mantle

268
00:15:10,801 --> 00:15:12,820
that we've never been able to drill into before.

269
00:15:12,960 --> 00:15:14,860
So we can study whole regions of the Earth

270
00:15:14,861 --> 00:15:17,040
that we really never have had access to

271
00:15:17,041 --> 00:15:18,680
until this ship came along.

272
00:15:20,300 --> 00:15:23,160
The Earth's mantle is truly virgin territory.

273
00:15:23,880 --> 00:15:25,980
One of the fundamental boundaries in the planet

274
00:15:25,981 --> 00:15:27,900
is the difference between the crust and the mantle.

275
00:15:28,180 --> 00:15:31,240
And we call that boundary between the two the moho layer.

276
00:15:31,241 --> 00:15:33,780
The moho is just that boundary between the rocks of the crust

277
00:15:33,880 --> 00:15:34,980
and the rocks of the Earth's mantle,

278
00:15:35,080 --> 00:15:37,540
which makes up something like 2 3rds of the planet.

279
00:15:37,640 --> 00:15:39,640
No one's ever taken a direct sample

280
00:15:39,740 --> 00:15:40,880
of the mantle of the Earth.

281
00:15:40,980 --> 00:15:44,080
So the ability to get there and study what those rocks are like

282
00:15:44,180 --> 00:15:48,000
is an important goal for Earth scientists.

283
00:15:48,100 --> 00:15:49,900
The GQ won't be the first drill ship

284
00:15:50,000 --> 00:15:51,500
to attempt this Herculean task.

285
00:15:54,700 --> 00:15:56,300
The first project, actually, that

286
00:15:56,400 --> 00:15:59,600
started scientific ocean drilling in 1963 or so

287
00:15:59,601 --> 00:16:01,140
called Project Moho.

288
00:16:01,240 --> 00:16:03,700
And the idea of that project was to go out into the ocean

289
00:16:03,800 --> 00:16:05,780
and actually drill through the Earth's crust

290
00:16:05,880 --> 00:16:08,040
and all the way into the mantle of the Earth.

291
00:16:08,140 --> 00:16:10,880
They severely underestimated how hard this was going to be,

292
00:16:10,980 --> 00:16:13,620
and they failed to actually get to the Earth's mantle.

293
00:16:17,180 --> 00:16:19,760
To reach the great depth of the Moho layer,

294
00:16:19,860 --> 00:16:22,460
the Chikyu is drilling in the middle of the ocean,

295
00:16:22,560 --> 00:16:24,020
where the Earth's crust is thinnest.

296
00:16:24,021 --> 00:16:28,560
And it's taking notes from the oil industry

297
00:16:28,660 --> 00:16:32,760
by incorporating a riser in the drilling process.

298
00:16:32,860 --> 00:16:35,560
The riser extends around the drill pipe from the ship

299
00:16:35,660 --> 00:16:38,500
to the sea floor.

300
00:16:38,600 --> 00:16:41,500
This shaft allows drilling mud to flush all the cuttings back

301
00:16:41,600 --> 00:16:44,600
up to the ship, shoring up unstable sediments

302
00:16:44,700 --> 00:16:47,280
and keeping the borehole clear.

303
00:16:47,380 --> 00:16:49,720
Prior to this, without a riser system,

304
00:16:49,820 --> 00:16:54,020
scientific ocean drilling has always been done just with a

305
00:16:54,021 --> 00:16:56,260
the drill string itself cutting the hole.

306
00:16:56,360 --> 00:16:58,100
Sea water is pumped down the drill string

307
00:16:58,200 --> 00:17:00,260
to flush the pieces of rock out of the hole,

308
00:17:00,360 --> 00:17:02,160
and they just pile up on the seafloor.

309
00:17:02,260 --> 00:17:05,640
That works fine for drilling, oh, several thousand feet.

310
00:17:05,740 --> 00:17:06,960
But as you get deeper and deeper,

311
00:17:07,060 --> 00:17:09,900
it gets harder and harder to maintain the hole that way.

312
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:12,300
So to go deeper, we need this riser system.

313
00:17:12,400 --> 00:17:15,680
So it can drill two or three, four times deeper

314
00:17:15,780 --> 00:17:18,940
than we've ever been able to drill in the ocean floor before.

315
00:17:19,040 --> 00:17:20,440
Even if the Cheekyuu succeeds

316
00:17:20,540 --> 00:17:23,180
in boring to the moho layer, we will have only

317
00:17:23,181 --> 00:17:27,180
to scratch the surface, as there's more than 3,000 miles

318
00:17:27,280 --> 00:17:29,280
to go in order to reach the Earth's inner core.

319
00:17:33,220 --> 00:17:36,420
But as the GQ begins to probe the Earth's crust,

320
00:17:36,520 --> 00:17:38,220
other scientific drilling devices

321
00:17:38,320 --> 00:17:40,940
are uncovering thousands of years of history

322
00:17:41,040 --> 00:17:42,040
through ice coring.

323
00:17:45,340 --> 00:17:47,880
One of the important things about ice coring

324
00:17:47,980 --> 00:17:49,980
is that you get a completely continuous record,

325
00:17:49,981 --> 00:17:52,440
because this kind of drilling is done,

326
00:17:52,540 --> 00:17:55,620
the surface temperature on the ice sheet never

327
00:17:55,720 --> 00:17:58,720
gets up above to the melting point or above.

328
00:17:58,820 --> 00:18:02,620
So all the snow that falls stays frozen.

329
00:18:02,720 --> 00:18:06,000
It never melts, and it just continues to pile up.

330
00:18:06,100 --> 00:18:08,440
To preserve the integrity of the cores,

331
00:18:08,540 --> 00:18:11,340
unique ice-coring drill bits are used.

332
00:18:11,440 --> 00:18:14,040
The idea of a core drilling system

333
00:18:14,140 --> 00:18:18,440
is to cut an annulus around a pillar of ice.

334
00:18:18,441 --> 00:18:23,440
The cutting is done by these knife blades here, which are sharp,

335
00:18:23,441 --> 00:18:27,440
and they act just like a carpenter's plane.

336
00:18:27,441 --> 00:18:31,440
As the drill rotates, it cuts down into the ice.

337
00:18:31,441 --> 00:18:36,440
If you have one piece rotating, you have to have something for it to provide resistance to that rotation.

338
00:18:36,441 --> 00:18:39,440
And that's what this anti-torque section does.

339
00:18:39,441 --> 00:18:43,440
The anti-torque section, these are springs that I can compress.

340
00:18:43,441 --> 00:18:48,880
They extend out and are pressed up against the wall of the hole.

341
00:18:48,980 --> 00:18:51,680
To date, scientists have been able to uncover

342
00:18:51,780 --> 00:18:54,920
over a million years of climate history through ice core.

343
00:18:58,060 --> 00:19:01,560
But drilling in ice is not limited to core sampling.

344
00:19:01,660 --> 00:19:04,200
Scientists are currently constructing a neutrino

345
00:19:04,300 --> 00:19:09,140
telescope at the South Pole, requiring them to drill 80 holes,

346
00:19:09,240 --> 00:19:13,440
a mile and a half each, into the ice sheet.

347
00:19:13,441 --> 00:19:15,560
We need to be in ice that's very clear.

348
00:19:16,060 --> 00:19:19,080
We want to be in what looks like cocktail ice.

349
00:19:19,820 --> 00:19:22,480
So cocktail ice, if you've ever noticed, is very clear.

350
00:19:22,720 --> 00:19:26,600
There's no cloudy bubbles in it like the kind of ice that you might make in a tray

351
00:19:26,601 --> 00:19:28,460
and then put in your freezer at home.

352
00:19:29,000 --> 00:19:33,780
So at the South Pole, due to compression and age and so forth,

353
00:19:33,781 --> 00:19:38,540
the ice that's about a kilometer and a half deep and deeper

354
00:19:38,541 --> 00:19:43,440
is we have found very clear and very suitable for our experiment.

355
00:19:43,441 --> 00:19:47,180
And it's one of the few places on the planet where there's

356
00:19:47,181 --> 00:19:51,180
really ice that we can use for this experiment.

357
00:19:51,181 --> 00:19:53,880
The Ice Cube engineers had to find

358
00:19:53,881 --> 00:19:57,520
an inexpensive and efficient way to drill a hole through the ice.

359
00:19:57,521 --> 00:19:59,100
Their method of choice?

360
00:19:59,101 --> 00:20:03,360
Hot water, or more precisely, an enhanced hot water drill.

361
00:20:03,361 --> 00:20:06,940
You just simply cannot drill a straight enough, deep enough,

362
00:20:06,941 --> 00:20:11,480
wide enough hole to meet our requirements of a couple feet

363
00:20:11,580 --> 00:20:15,380
across and over a mile and a half deep with a mechanical drill.

364
00:20:15,480 --> 00:20:16,880
So then what else do you do?

365
00:20:16,980 --> 00:20:21,360
Well, we've all probably had some experience actually

366
00:20:21,460 --> 00:20:24,320
melting ice with a water jet.

367
00:20:24,420 --> 00:20:26,520
Then in order to increase that efficiency,

368
00:20:26,620 --> 00:20:29,400
if you scale that water jet up and heat the water,

369
00:20:29,500 --> 00:20:32,840
you could actually very effectively melt ice.

370
00:20:32,841 --> 00:20:38,440
The enhanced hot water drill sprays 190 degree

371
00:20:38,540 --> 00:20:42,420
Fahrenheit water, pressurized to nearly 1,000 pounds per

372
00:20:42,520 --> 00:20:46,360
square inch, into the frozen path, melting the ice at a rate

373
00:20:46,460 --> 00:20:50,020
of over 3 and 1 half feet per minute.

374
00:20:50,120 --> 00:20:54,860
You direct this water through a flexible hose.

375
00:20:54,960 --> 00:20:59,740
So we have nearly 10,000 feet of this hose wrapped up

376
00:20:59,741 --> 00:21:02,940
on a very enormous hose reel.

377
00:21:03,040 --> 00:21:04,620
It's actually a very high-tech device.

378
00:21:04,720 --> 00:21:09,120
You just keep unspooling your hose as you go down the hole.

379
00:21:11,620 --> 00:21:14,860
With 200,000 gallons of ice melted per hole,

380
00:21:14,960 --> 00:21:18,460
each hole takes approximately 30 hours to drill.

381
00:21:18,560 --> 00:21:21,420
Eventually, because the South Pole ice is very cold,

382
00:21:21,520 --> 00:21:22,760
it's going to freeze back.

383
00:21:22,860 --> 00:21:25,660
Typically, the holes we drill, so they're a couple of feet

384
00:21:25,661 --> 00:21:29,800
a couple of feet across, they will freeze back solid

385
00:21:29,900 --> 00:21:33,600
in about 40 hours, more or less.

386
00:21:38,100 --> 00:21:40,900
After the hole is drilled, the Ice Cube team

387
00:21:41,000 --> 00:21:43,640
has to work against the clock to get all of their instrumentation

388
00:21:43,740 --> 00:21:45,720
installed before the hole disappears.

389
00:21:49,120 --> 00:21:51,860
Strings of basketball-sized optical modules

390
00:21:51,960 --> 00:21:54,960
will be frozen deep in these holes.

391
00:21:54,961 --> 00:21:59,160
will be used to detect neutrinos as they dart through the ice.

392
00:21:59,260 --> 00:22:01,100
They might be the key to our discovery

393
00:22:01,200 --> 00:22:06,000
of some of the most profound secrets of the universe.

394
00:22:06,100 --> 00:22:09,000
In ice, hot water may be able to cut it.

395
00:22:09,100 --> 00:22:10,800
But in other industries, you have

396
00:22:10,900 --> 00:22:13,620
to bring out the big guns to get the job done.

397
00:22:13,621 --> 00:22:14,520
Done.

398
00:22:23,900 --> 00:22:26,600
Deep in a West Virginia limestone mine,

399
00:22:26,700 --> 00:22:29,260
toils one of the largest tunnel and mining drill rigs

400
00:22:29,360 --> 00:22:37,580
in the United States, the Rocket Boomer XL2C jumbo drill.

401
00:22:37,680 --> 00:22:39,940
Drill rigs like this one are a critical component

402
00:22:40,040 --> 00:22:41,240
in drill and blast tunneling.

403
00:22:43,620 --> 00:22:45,820
Holes are drilled into the surface,

404
00:22:45,920 --> 00:22:47,720
and dynamite is inserted into the holes

405
00:22:47,820 --> 00:22:50,720
to blast material from the face of the solid rock.

406
00:22:50,820 --> 00:22:53,020
The Rockaboomer series of drill rigs

407
00:22:53,120 --> 00:22:56,860
can be used in just a wide variety of hard rock

408
00:22:56,960 --> 00:22:59,960
mining applications.

409
00:23:00,060 --> 00:23:02,880
The machine weighs approximately 90,000 pounds,

410
00:23:02,980 --> 00:23:07,680
as you see it, and has a reach of about 55 feet wide

411
00:23:07,780 --> 00:23:12,880
and about 45 feet high from a single setup.

412
00:23:12,881 --> 00:23:21,160
Double penetration allows the operator to drill 88 holes 18 feet deep into solid rock in less than three hours.

413
00:23:21,161 --> 00:23:27,300
On the end of the boom, you've got a drill feed, which will be feeding the drill bit in and out,

414
00:23:27,620 --> 00:23:29,300
in when we're drilling the hole.

415
00:23:29,840 --> 00:23:33,920
At the same time, there is a rock drill hammer on that feed,

416
00:23:34,180 --> 00:23:38,460
which will be giving an impact movement to the drill itself.

417
00:23:39,180 --> 00:23:41,740
And also at the same time, the bit will be rotating.

418
00:23:42,880 --> 00:23:47,480
In just a minute and a half, the drill penetrates 18 feet

419
00:23:47,580 --> 00:23:51,280
into solid rock, a length slightly longer than the height

420
00:23:51,380 --> 00:23:52,820
of a typical single-story home.

421
00:23:56,420 --> 00:24:00,040
From inside an enclosed cabin, the drill operator controls

422
00:24:00,140 --> 00:24:04,640
both arms simultaneously, using a preprogrammed computer

423
00:24:04,740 --> 00:24:07,140
console to guide his appendages.

424
00:24:07,240 --> 00:24:10,040
He has a joystick in the cabin, just like a video game

425
00:24:10,140 --> 00:24:11,040
that you have at home.

426
00:24:11,041 --> 00:24:11,880
game that you have at home.

427
00:24:11,881 --> 00:24:14,380
So you have a joystick, and it will locate that boom

428
00:24:14,381 --> 00:24:17,340
to the next hole in a precise area,

429
00:24:17,341 --> 00:24:19,540
in a precise angle that he wants to drill out to.

430
00:24:19,541 --> 00:24:24,440
And once he's done that, he'll start the drilling cycle again.

431
00:24:24,441 --> 00:24:26,820
With machinery like this rocket boomer,

432
00:24:26,821 --> 00:24:28,500
the modern driller's job description

433
00:24:28,501 --> 00:24:29,940
is drastically different from that

434
00:24:29,941 --> 00:24:32,880
of early 19th century drillers.

435
00:24:32,881 --> 00:24:35,220
And the manual drilling process made legendary

436
00:24:35,221 --> 00:24:40,400
by John Henry, the steel driving man.

437
00:24:40,401 --> 00:24:43,700
What we see here is a typical star drill and a couple

438
00:24:43,800 --> 00:24:45,140
of sledgehammers.

439
00:24:45,240 --> 00:24:48,700
In order to make the hole, one man would hold one of these drills

440
00:24:48,800 --> 00:24:51,340
and either two or three men would hammer the drill.

441
00:24:51,440 --> 00:24:53,740
It was relatively dangerous for the guy holding the drill,

442
00:24:53,840 --> 00:24:56,440
but clearly the men that had the sledgehammers were highly

443
00:24:56,540 --> 00:24:58,960
experienced and they didn't break his hand very often,

444
00:24:59,060 --> 00:25:01,860
hopefully.

445
00:25:01,960 --> 00:25:04,660
In the mid-1800s, engineers tunneling

446
00:25:04,760 --> 00:25:07,660
through Housik Mountain in northern Massachusetts

447
00:25:07,661 --> 00:25:10,400
were literally breaking new ground.

448
00:25:10,401 --> 00:25:13,400
At the time, the only type of drilling was hand drilling.

449
00:25:13,401 --> 00:25:15,900
But the rock at the Hoosig Tunnel was extremely strong.

450
00:25:15,901 --> 00:25:18,100
It was a granite type of rock.

451
00:25:18,101 --> 00:25:20,200
It was very difficult to drill by hand.

452
00:25:20,201 --> 00:25:23,200
And actually, the mechanics in Massachusetts

453
00:25:23,201 --> 00:25:28,920
developed a pneumatic drill that was driven by compressed air.

454
00:25:28,921 --> 00:25:31,020
Hoosig Tunnel engineers also introduced

455
00:25:31,021 --> 00:25:34,920
a more powerful blasting agent, nitroglycerin,

456
00:25:34,921 --> 00:25:40,220
replacing gunpowder to blast the rock face.

457
00:25:40,320 --> 00:25:42,860
The Hoosick Tunnel had also been the proving ground for another

458
00:25:42,960 --> 00:25:47,360
tunneling innovation. Instead of drilling small

459
00:25:47,460 --> 00:25:50,900
diameter holes in the rock for blasting agents, engineers

460
00:25:51,000 --> 00:25:54,240
experimented with a primitive tunnel boring machine to drill

461
00:25:54,340 --> 00:25:58,140
one tunnel-sized hole. The steel wasn't strong

462
00:25:58,240 --> 00:26:01,420
enough, the power source wasn't powerful enough, but they came

463
00:26:01,421 --> 00:26:05,520
They came up with a concept for using a full-face tunnel boring

464
00:26:05,521 --> 00:26:07,660
machine at the Hoosick Tunnel.

465
00:26:07,661 --> 00:26:10,100
The full-face boring machine ground 10 feet

466
00:26:10,101 --> 00:26:14,140
into the solid granite of Hoosick Mountain before it died.

467
00:26:14,141 --> 00:26:16,840
And this predated the actual development of these machines

468
00:26:16,841 --> 00:26:18,900
by over 100 years.

469
00:26:18,901 --> 00:26:21,980
The machines weren't actually ever developed

470
00:26:21,981 --> 00:26:26,280
as a practical apparatus until the 1950s.

471
00:26:26,281 --> 00:26:29,380
But these gentlemen were trying a similar concept at the Hoosick

472
00:26:29,381 --> 00:26:32,520
in the 1850s, so they were way ahead of their time.

473
00:26:35,920 --> 00:26:39,020
Today, tunnel boring machines, or TBMs,

474
00:26:39,120 --> 00:26:41,320
are standard issue in civil engineering tunneling

475
00:26:41,420 --> 00:26:44,900
projects, like this extension of the Los Angeles light rail

476
00:26:45,000 --> 00:26:45,800
system.

477
00:26:45,900 --> 00:26:48,740
This tunnel stretches a 7,000-foot long stretch

478
00:26:48,840 --> 00:26:53,000
underneath boil heights, twin tunnels, 6 and 1,5 meter

479
00:26:53,100 --> 00:26:56,500
in diameter, 21 feet in diameter.

480
00:26:56,600 --> 00:26:58,640
65 feet underground.

481
00:26:58,641 --> 00:27:02,040
The tool of choice, or rather tools of choice,

482
00:27:02,140 --> 00:27:06,040
are twin tunnel boring machines.

483
00:27:06,140 --> 00:27:10,220
The 344 foot long 1,025 ton beasts

484
00:27:10,320 --> 00:27:15,020
are expected to burrow through 50 feet of earth per day.

485
00:27:15,120 --> 00:27:17,620
It basically pushes and digests the ground,

486
00:27:17,720 --> 00:27:19,060
but it does it in a controlled manner

487
00:27:19,160 --> 00:27:23,640
to make sure that it always maintains a pressure within this

488
00:27:23,740 --> 00:27:27,460
cut ahead and plenum system so it's not greater than the,

489
00:27:27,461 --> 00:27:32,100
And so it's not less than the ground pressure around it.

490
00:27:32,200 --> 00:27:34,000
Each massive boring machine is equipped

491
00:27:34,100 --> 00:27:36,300
with a closed wheel cutting head that

492
00:27:36,400 --> 00:27:40,400
contains 18 tungsten carbide tipped disc cutters.

493
00:27:40,500 --> 00:27:43,700
Well, this cutter head weighs about 75 tons.

494
00:27:43,800 --> 00:27:49,220
It gets turned around and around about 2 RPM, maximum 3 RPM.

495
00:27:49,320 --> 00:27:52,120
But really what it does, it can turn both ways.

496
00:27:52,220 --> 00:27:55,660
It's one big disk of steel with a bunch of tools on it

497
00:27:55,661 --> 00:27:57,800
that dig the material.

498
00:27:57,900 --> 00:27:59,700
And what you see on here, the yellow things here

499
00:27:59,800 --> 00:28:01,260
are disc cutters.

500
00:28:01,360 --> 00:28:04,840
And then you see these openings here and here.

501
00:28:04,940 --> 00:28:07,480
These are bucket openings, which collect all the material

502
00:28:07,580 --> 00:28:11,440
and basically scoop it into the plenum of the cutter head.

503
00:28:11,540 --> 00:28:14,820
As the cutting head rotates, scraping into the ground,

504
00:28:14,920 --> 00:28:18,820
it is slowly pushed forward by 32 hydraulic jacks, which

505
00:28:18,920 --> 00:28:23,680
exert a total of 1,200 tons of thrust on the head.

506
00:28:23,681 --> 00:28:27,880
Basically, the machine will thrust off the tunnel lining

507
00:28:27,980 --> 00:28:31,680
and mine five feet, retract jacks in certain locations

508
00:28:31,780 --> 00:28:33,420
and start building that miner in the space

509
00:28:33,520 --> 00:28:36,160
that we just created.

510
00:28:36,260 --> 00:28:39,200
So basically, we push, mine, and then we build a ring,

511
00:28:39,300 --> 00:28:41,600
and push mine, build a ring.

512
00:28:41,700 --> 00:28:44,500
There's actually six nozzles on this gutter head.

513
00:28:44,600 --> 00:28:46,400
They're two inches in diameter.

514
00:28:46,500 --> 00:28:50,700
And what we pump through there is typically foam.

515
00:28:50,800 --> 00:28:52,540
The foam works essentially the same way

516
00:28:52,541 --> 00:28:56,140
as drilling mud. It mixes with the cuttings,

517
00:28:56,240 --> 00:28:59,740
making them the ideal consistency to move through huge screws

518
00:28:59,840 --> 00:29:05,680
away from the cutter head. At the helm of this giant

519
00:29:05,780 --> 00:29:08,680
is an operator who controls these screws and monitors

520
00:29:08,780 --> 00:29:11,500
the machine to make sure nothing goes wrong.

521
00:29:11,600 --> 00:29:14,900
I control the screws. I can speed them up,

522
00:29:15,000 --> 00:29:18,600
and I can empty that chamber out real fast.

523
00:29:18,601 --> 00:29:22,240
I can slow it down and keep that chamber full.

524
00:29:24,000 --> 00:29:28,680
If I slow it down, then my advance here

525
00:29:28,681 --> 00:29:30,520
where the machine's going to slow down.

526
00:29:31,840 --> 00:29:34,300
And eventually, if you slow it down too much,

527
00:29:35,020 --> 00:29:37,020
you'll just start shoving into the ground,

528
00:29:37,700 --> 00:29:38,960
and the ground's going to go somewhere.

529
00:29:39,060 --> 00:29:39,760
It's going to go up.

530
00:29:40,780 --> 00:29:43,800
So then you're going to create a hill up top.

531
00:29:44,440 --> 00:29:46,320
If I speed it up too fast,

532
00:29:46,321 --> 00:29:48,960
and I take that material out of the chamber fast,

533
00:29:49,960 --> 00:29:51,260
then the ground's going to come down

534
00:29:51,261 --> 00:29:52,180
and we'll have a sinkhole.

535
00:29:53,620 --> 00:29:54,900
Behind the drill head,

536
00:29:55,300 --> 00:29:57,340
these screws, or rotary augers,

537
00:29:57,760 --> 00:30:00,120
move the muck through the entire length of the machine.

538
00:30:00,680 --> 00:30:02,560
You don't even see the ground that you're mining

539
00:30:02,561 --> 00:30:05,700
until it's actually dropping out of the contained screw conveyor

540
00:30:05,701 --> 00:30:07,100
all the way at the end,

541
00:30:07,580 --> 00:30:10,000
some 200 feet from where it's actually coming out.

542
00:30:10,980 --> 00:30:12,520
Depending on the type of soil,

543
00:30:13,040 --> 00:30:14,740
a tunnel boring machine like this one

544
00:30:14,741 --> 00:30:18,380
of excavating up to 20,000 tons of material a day.

545
00:30:22,280 --> 00:30:24,820
Today's high-tech tunnel boring machines practically

546
00:30:24,880 --> 00:30:26,580
run themselves.

547
00:30:26,660 --> 00:30:29,520
And in some industries, engineers have taken drilling

548
00:30:29,580 --> 00:30:34,100
even one step further, with robots completely taking over

549
00:30:34,160 --> 00:30:35,300
the boring task.

550
00:30:35,301 --> 00:30:36,001
task.

551
00:30:43,000 --> 00:30:46,040
On the Sea Ray boat's factory floor,

552
00:30:46,041 --> 00:30:49,040
laser-guided robots wielding drills are revolutionizing

553
00:30:49,041 --> 00:30:52,380
the boating industry. They function as both the drill

554
00:30:52,381 --> 00:30:55,180
and the drill operator. What you see here

555
00:30:55,181 --> 00:31:01,200
are two ABB IRB 6500 robots mounted on rails,

556
00:31:01,201 --> 00:31:06,840
and they run off of one controller.

557
00:31:06,940 --> 00:31:09,140
The robots perform a choreographed drilling

558
00:31:09,240 --> 00:31:13,040
sequence, unique to each boat style, maneuvering around the

559
00:31:13,140 --> 00:31:15,340
vessel effortlessly.

560
00:31:15,440 --> 00:31:18,680
The robots have six axes, and also being mounted on this

561
00:31:18,780 --> 00:31:20,420
track, that's an additional axis.

562
00:31:20,520 --> 00:31:22,480
So it's actually seven axes.

563
00:31:22,580 --> 00:31:26,920
What that allows is a lot of mobility of the arm to articulate

564
00:31:27,020 --> 00:31:28,800
in whatever position it needs to.

565
00:31:28,801 --> 00:31:30,960
And also, with the seventh axis,

566
00:31:31,060 --> 00:31:33,340
the robot can travel back and forth

567
00:31:33,440 --> 00:31:39,540
and cover a large area and work on a part as large as this.

568
00:31:39,640 --> 00:31:42,240
The robots have 40-horsepower spindles

569
00:31:42,340 --> 00:31:45,780
that can turn the carbide drill bits up to 18,000 revolutions

570
00:31:45,880 --> 00:31:47,320
per minute.

571
00:31:49,580 --> 00:31:55,000
And their precision and accuracy are unparalleled.

572
00:31:55,100 --> 00:31:57,900
The guy doing it by hand, he has to measure everything out,

573
00:31:57,901 --> 00:31:58,940
which takes time.

574
00:31:58,941 --> 00:32:00,700
He has to make sure we can, you know,

575
00:32:00,701 --> 00:32:02,600
he can read the tape measure correctly

576
00:32:02,601 --> 00:32:05,340
and get everything laid out to the correct specs.

577
00:32:05,341 --> 00:32:08,440
With the robots, we don't have to do that.

578
00:32:08,441 --> 00:32:10,640
By hand, on average, it would take about an hour

579
00:32:10,641 --> 00:32:15,760
to lay out the boat completely and drill it for all the options.

580
00:32:15,761 --> 00:32:17,060
With the setup that we have now,

581
00:32:17,061 --> 00:32:20,960
we're doing it in about 20 minutes.

582
00:32:20,961 --> 00:32:23,300
Plus, the robots always show up.

583
00:32:23,301 --> 00:32:27,400
They don't need lunch breaks, and they never complain.

584
00:32:27,401 --> 00:32:30,160
It's nice to see the work that you put into it

585
00:32:30,161 --> 00:32:32,900
basically at a desk, and then you come out here,

586
00:32:32,901 --> 00:32:34,740
and it does everything that you want it to do.

587
00:32:34,741 --> 00:32:36,640
For the most part, it's dead on.

588
00:32:36,641 --> 00:32:38,040
It's so precise.

589
00:32:38,041 --> 00:32:44,160
And you get that consistency that humans can't provide.

590
00:32:44,161 --> 00:32:46,280
Though technically impressive,

591
00:32:46,281 --> 00:32:49,020
these robotic drills employ some of the same principles

592
00:32:49,021 --> 00:32:53,020
as the earliest hand drills.

593
00:32:53,021 --> 00:32:55,320
Some of the earliest drills that we have

594
00:32:55,321 --> 00:32:57,860
are the stone tools of native people.

595
00:32:57,861 --> 00:33:01,200
This is an example from early America,

596
00:33:01,201 --> 00:33:04,660
several thousand years old, dating to the Archaic period.

597
00:33:04,661 --> 00:33:08,560
Basically, a stone perforator pointed, napped to a point,

598
00:33:08,561 --> 00:33:12,140
that would have been inserted into a wooden handle,

599
00:33:12,141 --> 00:33:15,480
lashed together, so that you would, through your hands,

600
00:33:15,481 --> 00:33:17,980
spin the tool back and forth and create a hole.

601
00:33:17,981 --> 00:33:20,640
And so, at its most basic level, that is a drill.

602
00:33:20,641 --> 00:33:23,720
A similar kind of tool is the bow drill.

603
00:33:23,721 --> 00:33:26,620
Again, a kind of chisel or very simple point

604
00:33:26,720 --> 00:33:30,420
mounted on a spindle with a bow that looks a lot like a violin bow.

605
00:33:30,520 --> 00:33:34,960
I can't do this exactly because it's impossible with the age of the material

606
00:33:35,060 --> 00:33:37,060
to actually twist this around.

607
00:33:37,160 --> 00:33:40,780
But with the string on the bow twisted around the cylinder

608
00:33:40,880 --> 00:33:43,980
or around the spindle and moving it back and forth

609
00:33:44,080 --> 00:33:49,020
would create a spinning motion, a reciprocating spinning motion

610
00:33:49,120 --> 00:33:50,920
that would create your hole.

611
00:33:50,921 --> 00:33:53,220
The bow drill was known in antiquity.

612
00:33:53,221 --> 00:33:56,220
It certainly was a tool that was known to the Egyptians

613
00:33:56,221 --> 00:33:58,420
and the Romans, for example.

614
00:33:58,421 --> 00:34:02,560
A similar kind of tool is the pump drill, which is also

615
00:34:02,561 --> 00:34:05,600
a back-and-forth reciprocating motion,

616
00:34:05,601 --> 00:34:09,440
but relies instead of pulling it back and forth on the bow,

617
00:34:09,441 --> 00:34:11,980
you are pumping up and down.

618
00:34:11,981 --> 00:34:14,080
By the early 15th century,

619
00:34:14,081 --> 00:34:16,940
a vastly more powerful hand drill had been developed

620
00:34:16,941 --> 00:34:20,920
that allowed for continuous rotary motion, the brace.

621
00:34:20,921 --> 00:34:24,220
Now you have a drill, or more popularly,

622
00:34:24,320 --> 00:34:30,360
a boring tool, a bit, moving in a constant and single direction.

623
00:34:30,460 --> 00:34:32,800
And the way these tools worked is that they relied

624
00:34:32,900 --> 00:34:35,340
on interchangeable pads, or chucks,

625
00:34:35,440 --> 00:34:38,480
we might call them today, in which one could insert

626
00:34:38,580 --> 00:34:42,700
a variety of sizes of bits.

627
00:34:42,800 --> 00:34:44,640
By the time the brace had made its appearance

628
00:34:44,740 --> 00:34:47,340
in the tradesman's kit, the bit itself assumed

629
00:34:47,440 --> 00:34:49,980
many shapes and sizes.

630
00:34:49,981 --> 00:34:53,680
The big advance with drilling or more properly boring

631
00:34:53,780 --> 00:34:58,660
technology was the innovation of the twist or spiral bit.

632
00:34:58,760 --> 00:35:02,200
With the screw, not only do you bore that hole,

633
00:35:02,300 --> 00:35:05,760
but you also have the additional advantage of the screw

634
00:35:05,860 --> 00:35:08,960
carrying the shavings back up and out of the holes.

635
00:35:11,400 --> 00:35:15,640
Drilling technology took a giant leap forward in 1895

636
00:35:15,740 --> 00:35:19,100
when a radical new hand drill invented by Wilhelm Fein

637
00:35:19,101 --> 00:35:21,600
Germany came on the scene.

638
00:35:21,700 --> 00:35:24,380
It was the first electric drill, and though it probably weighs

639
00:35:24,480 --> 00:35:27,340
10 to 12 pounds or so, it was real easy to use for the

640
00:35:27,440 --> 00:35:30,680
average person who wasn't having to hand crank that bitten brace

641
00:35:30,780 --> 00:35:31,840
all the time.

642
00:35:31,940 --> 00:35:33,340
It did get kind of tired of the hands.

643
00:35:33,440 --> 00:35:36,480
It was a bit ungainly, but it was real easy for the average

644
00:35:36,580 --> 00:35:39,160
person to be able to get behind it, to actually put his chest

645
00:35:39,260 --> 00:35:43,060
on here and actually push into the work.

646
00:35:43,160 --> 00:35:45,600
In the early 20th century, Black & Decker

647
00:35:45,700 --> 00:35:49,100
made the portable electric hand drill even easier for the

648
00:35:49,101 --> 00:35:54,100
So along came a couple of guys named Duncan Black and Alonzo Decker,

649
00:35:54,101 --> 00:35:58,100
and they had this brilliant idea that they got from the Colt .45 pistol,

650
00:35:58,101 --> 00:36:00,100
the pistol grip.

651
00:36:00,101 --> 00:36:10,100
And right here is, I mean, this is very much like the original Black and Decker power drill,

652
00:36:10,101 --> 00:36:14,100
which came out, they actually came up with the idea in 1914.

653
00:36:14,860 --> 00:36:17,560
They took it to market in 1916.

654
00:36:17,680 --> 00:36:20,880
In 1919, they took a van around the country,

655
00:36:20,960 --> 00:36:22,860
and they went to hardware stores everywhere,

656
00:36:23,120 --> 00:36:24,880
and they demonstrated these things,

657
00:36:24,900 --> 00:36:26,240
and people were just amazed.

658
00:36:27,120 --> 00:36:29,620
Their invention would revolutionize the industry

659
00:36:29,621 --> 00:36:32,560
and put a power drill within reach of handymen everywhere.

660
00:36:33,380 --> 00:36:36,700
People were happy for, you know, 50 years with an electric drill,

661
00:36:36,701 --> 00:36:38,960
but then suddenly they started realizing

662
00:36:38,961 --> 00:36:42,640
they were tethered to wherever there was an electrical outlet.

663
00:36:42,641 --> 00:36:46,640
So in 1961, Black & Decker introduced to the world

664
00:36:46,740 --> 00:36:47,980
the cordless drill.

665
00:36:48,080 --> 00:36:49,880
They actually just took regular batteries,

666
00:36:49,980 --> 00:36:52,020
and they lumped them together, and they used that to power

667
00:36:52,120 --> 00:36:53,180
drill.

668
00:36:53,280 --> 00:36:55,060
It had very little power, but it was able to start using

669
00:36:55,160 --> 00:36:58,160
batteries instead of cords to do the same kind of drilling.

670
00:36:58,260 --> 00:37:04,020
And we progressed in that all the way up to this type of thing.

671
00:37:04,120 --> 00:37:07,700
And this is a relatively modern drill.

672
00:37:07,800 --> 00:37:10,560
Today, power drills are the most commonly purchased

673
00:37:10,561 --> 00:37:15,560
hand-held power tool in the world.

674
00:37:15,660 --> 00:37:17,760
They employ drill bits that are made of everything

675
00:37:17,860 --> 00:37:22,300
from steel to cobalt, tungsten, and titanium.

676
00:37:22,400 --> 00:37:25,800
Some even have edges impregnated with diamond dust.

677
00:37:25,900 --> 00:37:29,020
And they come in every size and shape imaginable.

678
00:37:29,120 --> 00:37:34,320
You can find a drill bit to put a hole through any material.

679
00:37:34,420 --> 00:37:39,220
But the drill bit of the future may not be a bit at all.

680
00:37:40,560 --> 00:37:45,560
It's straight out of a science fiction movie,

681
00:37:45,561 --> 00:37:48,560
using lasers to vaporize matter.

682
00:37:48,561 --> 00:37:51,560
But then again, the next generation of drilling technology

683
00:37:51,561 --> 00:37:55,560
got its start from the Star Wars missile defense program.

684
00:37:55,561 --> 00:37:59,560
In the mid-1990s, the Star Wars program was sort of winding down,

685
00:37:59,561 --> 00:38:02,560
and some people in the drilling business thought,

686
00:38:02,561 --> 00:38:05,560
well, it would be a good idea maybe to experiment with those

687
00:38:05,561 --> 00:38:07,560
Star Wars lasers and see if they could be used

688
00:38:07,561 --> 00:38:09,560
to drill oil and gas wells.

689
00:38:09,561 --> 00:38:12,800
So they went around to the Air Force and the Army

690
00:38:12,900 --> 00:38:14,300
and used some of their lasers.

691
00:38:14,400 --> 00:38:18,300
And they found that, sure, the lasers are powerful enough

692
00:38:18,400 --> 00:38:22,300
to destroy rocks or put holes in practically anything.

693
00:38:22,400 --> 00:38:25,080
So the idea that Star Wars lasers would be useful

694
00:38:25,180 --> 00:38:28,840
was demonstrated, but it was decided that really it was

695
00:38:28,940 --> 00:38:32,220
more appropriate to use industrial lasers.

696
00:38:32,320 --> 00:38:34,980
The US government's Argonne National Lab

697
00:38:35,080 --> 00:38:37,180
has decided to develop the technology.

698
00:38:37,181 --> 00:38:40,180
We found that there are basically three mechanisms

699
00:38:40,280 --> 00:38:43,120
for heating rocks with the laser.

700
00:38:43,220 --> 00:38:46,180
The first mechanism is when you just put the beam on

701
00:38:46,280 --> 00:38:50,820
at a low intensity, the rock temperature rises,

702
00:38:50,920 --> 00:38:54,840
and it sort of shatters, like what happens

703
00:38:54,940 --> 00:38:57,740
if you take a hot glass and you put it in cold water.

704
00:38:57,840 --> 00:38:58,940
It's going to shatter.

705
00:38:59,040 --> 00:39:01,040
That's the thermal fracture mechanism.

706
00:39:01,140 --> 00:39:03,640
If you continue to put the laser beam on for a longer

707
00:39:03,740 --> 00:39:06,180
period of time, the rock starts to melt.

708
00:39:06,181 --> 00:39:09,220
And if you leave it on long enough, that melted rock

709
00:39:09,320 --> 00:39:13,320
starts to boil or vaporize.

710
00:39:13,420 --> 00:39:15,420
To date, Claude Reed and his team

711
00:39:15,520 --> 00:39:18,120
have successfully developed the thermal fracturing method.

712
00:39:21,700 --> 00:39:24,540
The laser produces an invisible vertical beam

713
00:39:24,640 --> 00:39:26,760
the size of a mechanical pencil lead,

714
00:39:26,860 --> 00:39:30,540
which shatters the rock fragments.

715
00:39:30,640 --> 00:39:34,500
An air nozzle blows the fragments off the surface.

716
00:39:34,501 --> 00:39:40,400
As each layer is removed, then the laser head would go down to the next level, remove

717
00:39:40,401 --> 00:39:42,700
the material, then down to the next level.

718
00:39:42,701 --> 00:39:50,580
And it just marches downward, step by step, removing each layer of material and taking

719
00:39:50,581 --> 00:39:52,360
it out of the hole.

720
00:39:52,361 --> 00:39:56,020
Laser drilling will have several advantages over conventional drilling.

721
00:39:56,021 --> 00:40:01,860
It's expected to cost less, work faster, and be more precise.

722
00:40:01,861 --> 00:40:04,020
But the technology has a few more hurdles to clear

723
00:40:04,120 --> 00:40:07,100
before going out in the field.

724
00:40:07,200 --> 00:40:11,360
In 10 years or more, laser drills could and probably will

725
00:40:11,460 --> 00:40:14,900
replace all oil and gas well drilling.

726
00:40:15,000 --> 00:40:19,120
There's no real fundamental reason why laser drills

727
00:40:19,220 --> 00:40:21,320
can't do that job.

728
00:40:21,420 --> 00:40:25,520
They can penetrate any rock material.

729
00:40:25,620 --> 00:40:30,120
They can melt and vaporize any material.

730
00:40:30,121 --> 00:40:32,760
On a much smaller scale, laser drills have already proven

731
00:40:32,761 --> 00:40:34,960
themselves in other high-tech fields.

732
00:40:38,960 --> 00:40:42,160
At DDI Global in Anaheim, California,

733
00:40:42,161 --> 00:40:44,840
laser drilling is essential to the manufacture of printed

734
00:40:44,841 --> 00:40:48,480
circuit boards, or PCBs.

735
00:40:48,481 --> 00:40:52,040
Without PCBs, you would not have your consumer electronics

736
00:40:52,041 --> 00:40:57,320
at home, appliances, could be TVs, computers, cell phones.

737
00:40:57,321 --> 00:40:59,280
Just in general, any electronic device today

738
00:40:59,281 --> 00:41:02,440
has got a circuit board in it.

739
00:41:02,441 --> 00:41:04,040
Circuit boards consist of anywhere

740
00:41:04,041 --> 00:41:07,840
from two to 40 or more layers of information conduits,

741
00:41:07,841 --> 00:41:10,780
etched on copper film, which is highly conductive

742
00:41:10,781 --> 00:41:14,060
of electrical current.

743
00:41:14,061 --> 00:41:16,460
Holes are drilled in order to create interconnections

744
00:41:16,461 --> 00:41:18,800
between the various layers.

745
00:41:18,801 --> 00:41:20,320
We took a cell phone board, for example,

746
00:41:20,321 --> 00:41:22,540
even just like something like the PDA,

747
00:41:22,541 --> 00:41:24,560
or the next generation mobile phones.

748
00:41:24,561 --> 00:41:25,800
Typically, in a cell phone today,

749
00:41:25,801 --> 00:41:29,540
we have anywhere from 15,000 to 20,000 holes or greater.

750
00:41:30,040 --> 00:41:33,120
And it's just increasing because, again, it comes down to functionality.

751
00:41:33,600 --> 00:41:35,340
You want a phone to be a phone.

752
00:41:35,460 --> 00:41:36,660
You want a phone to be an email.

753
00:41:36,840 --> 00:41:38,840
You want a phone to be our personal friend.

754
00:41:41,100 --> 00:41:43,680
These holes, or microvias as they're called,

755
00:41:43,880 --> 00:41:47,040
were traditionally created by devices like this mechanical drill.

756
00:41:47,940 --> 00:41:51,300
The real estate offered on a circuit board is shrinking.

757
00:41:51,301 --> 00:42:01,420
So what we're doing is offering smaller holes on the circuit boards to make the Z-axis connection.

758
00:42:02,880 --> 00:42:08,640
The laser allows us to make much smaller holes than we can with, say, a mechanical drill.

759
00:42:09,660 --> 00:42:12,840
And that gives us higher density of the circuit board.

760
00:42:13,940 --> 00:42:17,520
Each flicker of light you see is a hole that has just been created.

761
00:42:17,521 --> 00:42:22,820
Using a laser beam results in microscopic holes drilled

762
00:42:22,821 --> 00:42:26,760
extremely accurately and at very high speeds.

763
